        <h2>Summary / Description</h2>
        <div class="description">
          The third International Space Station (ISS) crew, Expedition Commander Frank L. Culbertson, Jr., Flight Engineer Mikhail Tyurin, and Soyuz Commander Vladimir Dezhurov, launched aboard the Shuttle Discovery on the STS-105 mission August 10, 2001. Expedition Three's four-month mission was highlighted by continued scientific research and the addition of a new Russian docking port, which serves as an additional docking port for visiting Soyuz and Progress spacecraft and as an airlock from which Russian segment spacewalks can be conducted. Three spacewalks were performed during Expedition Three to electrically mate the docking compartment to Zvezda and to install additional equipment to the exterior of the module.
<P>
In addition to two Progress dockings, one "taxi" flight with a crew of two Russian cosmonauts and French Space Agency (CNES) cosmonaut occurred during Expedition Three. The "taxi" crew spent a week aboard the space station conducting joint investigations and a host of CNES-sponsored experiments. 
<P>
Research facilities were delivered to the station during Expedition Three in the Leonardo Multipurpose Logistics Module. These included two EXPRESS (Expedite the Processing of Experiments to the Space Station) racks, which transport, store, and support experiments on the station, and the Cellular Biotechnology Operations Support System (CBOSS). CBOSS was designed to augment cell growth and research aboard the station providing preservation, temperature regulation, and proper stowage of specimens during delivery, experimentation and return to Earth. 
<P>
Life sciences experiments and payloads that began during Expedition Three were:
<P>
<UL> 
<LI><B>Xenon 1</B>, a study of blood pressure problems and fainting that may occur when astronauts return to Earth.</LI>
<LI><B>Pulmonary Function in Flight</B> focuses on measuring changes in the evenness of gas exchange in the lungs and on detecting changes in respiratory muscle strength.</LI>
<LI><B>Dynamically Controlled Protein Crystal Growth</B> experiment and the <B>Advanced Protein Crystallization Facility</B> for growing biological materials that may lead to insights in the fields of medicine and agriculture.</LI>
<LI><B>Renal Stone</B> experiment examining the increased risk of kidney stone development during and immediately after space flight.</LI> 
</UL>
<P>
<P>
Life sciences experiments continuing into Expedition Three from earlier missions were: 
<P>
<UL>
<LI><B>Hoffman Reflex </B> to study changes in neurovestibular function with the goal of determining if exercise could be made more effective during long-term space flight.</LI>
<LI><B>Bonner Ball Neutron Detector</B> to develop safety measures against neutron radiation to protect crewmembers during long-duration space flights.</LI>
<LI><B>Crew Interactions</B> to identify and characterize interpersonal and cultural factors that may affect crew and ground support personnel performance during space station missions.</LI>
<LI><B>Sub-regional Assessment of Bone Loss in the Axial Skeleton in Long-Term Space Flight</B> to measure crewmembers' bone loss and postflight recovery.</LI>
</UL>
<P>
Other experiments flown for the first time were Dreamtime, a high definition television camcorder on the station, which is part of a public/private partnership to upgrade NASA's equipment to next generation HDTV, and the Materials International Space Station Experiment, which tested the durability of hundreds of samples ranging from lubricants to solar cell technologies. 
<P>
The Expedition Three crewmembers returned to Earth on December 17, 2001, aboard the Space Shuttle Discovery at the completion of the STS-105 mission.
        </div>

